{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"5ca11a74e4c8118e","type":"text","text":"# Higher education is broken. This map asks what could work better.\n\nA public research map by [Rodrigo Dias](https://rgo.pt/).","x":3860,"y":0,"width":700,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"4cad20a33bfb10d0","type":"text","text":"This is a **🚧 work in progress**: a structured record of firsthand observations, open questions, possible alternatives, outside perspectives, and practical advice for navigating the current system. It is not a finished blueprint.","x":3860,"y":260,"width":700,"height":120,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"4b1e0b07d7714b2e","type":"text","text":"## How to read this map\n\n1. What is wrong with current higher education\n2. The roles university bundles together\n3. Teaching-quality failure modes\n4. Principles and experiments for a better system\n5. Research notes and outside perspectives\n6. Advice for students navigating the current system","x":4320,"y":960,"width":640,"height":400,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"dc289ba7f11020cd","type":"text","text":"### The core grading question\n\nWhat are grades actually measuring: how much you learned, how much you know now, how you performed at one moment, or where you sit relative to an average?","x":0,"y":700,"width":640,"height":180,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"0975a4fb1a3863fb","type":"text","text":"## What even are grades?\n\n**What might they measure?**\n- How good you become at a specific subject by the end.\n- How good you are throughout the semester.\n- How much you can build with the material.\n- How much you know.\n- How many concepts you understand.\n\n**Compared with what?**\n- The average?\n- The median?\n- A fixed baseline, such as 60%?\n- The students in the current year, or everyone since the subject began? Can the meaning change drastically between cohorts?\n\nThe answer seems to depend on the subject, the teacher, and the grading design—which is precisely the problem: the same number can mean radically different things.","x":0,"y":940,"width":640,"height":520},
		{"id":"558743d14da33de0","type":"text","text":"### Teach knowledge with epistemic humility\n\nSometimes we are taught a human-made way of solving a problem as though it exhausts every possible approach. Those systems were designed by fallible people who could not have considered every solution.\n\nEven the notation used for laws of physics is human-made and might be improved. Our best physical laws may also be incomplete until an unfamiliar phenomenon reveals their limits.\n\nIn my theological framing, God made reality; humans are trying to describe it accurately enough to build bridges and do useful work. Teach these as powerful models and approximations, not as if every human formulation were exact and infallible.","x":0,"y":1520,"width":640,"height":340},
		{"id":"6d5efdb323892f0d","type":"text","text":"# 1 — What is wrong with current higher education?\n\n> [!context] These observations come from my experience as a Computer Science & Engineering student at [FEUP](https://fe.up.pt/) in Portugal. Your experience may vary.","x":1440,"y":420,"width":1360,"height":220,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"40208c834c2bd7ab","type":"text","text":"### Multiple-choice exams are tiny, noisy samples\n\nSingle-answer multiple-choice tests are the most common way my expertise is evaluated, but:\n- The sample size is too small.\n- Good questions and believable distractors are hard to write, and many teachers do not write them well. Often, even without knowing much, you can eliminate implausible options because the wrong answers were built too obviously around the correct one.\n- They test what I get right—and expose selected gaps—rather than representing everything I actually know. That may be the wrong way to test knowledge.\n- Compressing four months of work into roughly 20 choices over two hours is unrealistic.\n- A particularly good or bad day can swing the result.","x":720,"y":700,"width":640,"height":420},
		{"id":"f2d74526e5c2ed3f","type":"text","text":"### Evaluate projects while they are being built\n\nProjects are valuable, but making the final result or presentation the only evaluation point is a huge miss. A student can take a wrong turn early, fail to ask the teacher in time, and discover the consequences only at the end.\n\nEvaluate the construction process—continuously or through sprints. Feedback from professors can then redirect the work, improve the final result, and make the student actually learn while building instead of merely receiving a grade at the end.","x":720,"y":1180,"width":640,"height":280},
		{"id":"996851582ccb4b82","type":"text","text":"### Group work needs contribution transparency\n\nIn group projects, it is hard to know how much each student contributed. Group work matters, but the process should be more transparent: GitHub commit history, some other event log, and more open conversations could help.\n\nI am not sure exactly how this should be implemented. It would also matter less if the grading system were not so relentlessly numerical.","x":720,"y":1520,"width":640,"height":220},
		{"id":"d0371e28c3de8ce8","type":"text","text":"### Fully prescribed projects train obedience\n\n\"Factory-monkey\" projects should not exist as-is. This may be specific to my course, but in one programming group project every required step was laid out in front of us.\n\nThat kind of project creates workers who do only what they are told. Creativity and critical thinking should receive much more emphasis.","x":1440,"y":1460,"width":640,"height":220},
		{"id":"6237f28886640a26","type":"text","text":"### Assessment rarely closes the learning loop\n\nAfter failing an exam, many students care more about the failure than about the fact that they do not understand the topic. They do not use the result to learn from their mistakes.\n\nThe problem can be even worse for students who pass. If you get slightly above the grade you wanted or expected, you may never check the correction—if it is provided at all. In my experience, exam review was often possible but so inconvenient that even I rarely attended: I had to travel to the university at a specific time merely to inspect the exam, usually without taking notes. The process felt designed to catch marking mistakes, not to recover the knowledge I had missed.\n\nStudents then forget the material within days or weeks—again, this is personal experience and may vary. Tests, and much of the current system, seem to optimize for grading rather than for leaving with durable knowledge that can be applied in real life.","x":1440,"y":700,"width":640,"height":460},
		{"id":"c9b408acb26e2f74","type":"text","text":"### Time pressure can distort exams\n\nSome exams become a race against an arbitrary clock. Time limits can make sense for certain material, but in many cases an exam only barely feels doable within the time the teacher set. Give students enough time for the task the exam is meant to measure.","x":1440,"y":1220,"width":640,"height":180},
		{"id":"206595ff6f08b7f0","type":"text","text":"### Grades dominate the incentive system\n\nThe system reduces too much to numbers. Grades become almost everything students are pushed to pursue.\n\nProjects and other incentives do exist, but they are rarely given comparable importance.","x":2160,"y":700,"width":640,"height":180},
		{"id":"1cd66df557247172","type":"text","text":"### People are more than numbers\n\nPeople become numbers, but each person's abilities are unique. That sounds cliché because it is true: a single number cannot definitively describe how much someone knows about a broad or narrow topic.\n\nRepresent abilities as qualities instead: **what** a person knows, how they apply it, and how they learned it.","x":2160,"y":940,"width":640,"height":220},
		{"id":"6c401365341b90ad","type":"text","text":"### Make everyday human learning normal\n\nMore emphasis should be placed on talking, interacting, and learning through other people. I do not mean adding more corporate networking events—there are already plenty. I mean making it normal for students to talk with one another and with the teacher, and to learn personally from other people every day.\n\nThat is not the norm now. Students often seem willing to engage only inside the classroom and only during the time assigned to them—and even \"willing\" is generous.","x":2880,"y":700,"width":640,"height":260},
		{"id":"b04d9431185f455b","type":"text","text":"### Pressure produces badly matched study choices\n\nMany people seem forced into studying, see no other option, or feel they must pursue a subject they neither care about nor want to study.","x":2880,"y":1020,"width":640,"height":160,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"2557aee20da9a019","type":"text","text":"### Studying for the exam is not the same as learning\n\nPeople often study only for exams. As they encounter material, they should gradually know it, use it, and become curious about it. Many students do not, because they simply do not care about the subject.","x":2880,"y":1240,"width":640,"height":180,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"87c5f0e2b2765e28","type":"text","text":"### Boring delivery\n\nSubjects are too often taught in a boring way.","x":2880,"y":1480,"width":300,"height":140,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"707c1a2bc3da8395","type":"text","text":"### Badly matched choices\n\nDegree choices can be completely mis-chosen.","x":3220,"y":1480,"width":300,"height":140,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"bcd970b900946a35","type":"text","text":"### Prioritize problem solving and information finding\n\nGive more emphasis to framing problems and discovering solutions. With the internet—and now LLMs—nobody needs to recall every formula in a tenth of a second. Finding, evaluating, and using information are essential skills.","x":3600,"y":700,"width":640,"height":200},
		{"id":"7b6b27050b31f4b7","type":"text","text":"### More agency\n\nRequire less by default and offer more meaningful options that students can choose.","x":3600,"y":960,"width":640,"height":140},
		{"id":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","type":"text","text":"# 2 — University is a bundle of roles\n\nUniversity is not one thing. It combines many jobs and expectations inside a single institutional bubble; each role creates different value, incentives, and conflicts.","x":5760,"y":420,"width":1360,"height":160,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"ac9816c70d0b5369","type":"text","text":"### Learning\n\nOften treated as university's sole purpose, learning is actually one role among several—and the main role this map tries to rethink.","x":4320,"y":640,"width":460,"height":160},
		{"id":"e40b0f5ed7229998","type":"text","text":"### Employment and infrastructure\n\nUniversities provide jobs for teachers and for everyone needed to maintain their facilities and infrastructure.","x":4840,"y":640,"width":460,"height":140},
		{"id":"cba99104a7a73702","type":"text","text":"### Filtering and sorting for society\n\nWhen evaluating a job candidate, employers may ask: Did they attend university? Which one? Did they finish? With what grade? They trust the filtering performed by the institution.","x":5360,"y":640,"width":460,"height":200},
		{"id":"e9a750c69b8663e5","type":"text","text":"### Maturation and a holding space\n\nUniversity partly supervises and matures young adults who still do not know what to do with their lives.","x":5880,"y":640,"width":460,"height":180},
		{"id":"fd949e63862715b6","type":"text","text":"### Leaving home\n\nUniversity gives people a reason to leave the house—or to get away from their parents.","x":6400,"y":640,"width":460,"height":140},
		{"id":"8f8689ee64149323","type":"text","text":"### Socializing, parties, events, and networking\n\n- Some people simply get drunk.\n- Students cannot always choose whom they spend time with, learn from, or join for group projects.","x":6920,"y":640,"width":460,"height":220},
		{"id":"97512e905ca4f89a","type":"text","text":"### An insurance policy\n\nFor parents and students, university can be the safe default:\n- “Just go to university or college.”\n- “I do not know what I am going to do, so I will just go to university.”","x":7440,"y":640,"width":460,"height":220},
		{"id":"c3e69b85cc590897","type":"text","text":"### Research and knowledge production\n\n- Drives university prestige and rankings.\n- Staff are often hired and promoted for research output rather than teaching ability.\n- Knowledge is often locked behind paywalls or developed as R&D for external entities.\n- This role can conflict directly with the **Learning** role.","x":7960,"y":640,"width":460,"height":300},
		{"id":"eb8a2d105577f479","type":"text","text":"# 3 — Teaching quality and institutional practice\n\nBad teachers are too common—in my experience especially in private institutions, but also in public ones. The following taxonomy captures clear warning signs.","x":0,"y":1900,"width":1360,"height":140,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d3882d06cefb8d2c","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/q7MXwhIGJvA","x":1440,"y":1900,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"1927eaf2ab638174","type":"text","text":"Attitude/mindset issues","x":0,"y":2320,"width":640,"height":100,"color":"#39c3fe"},
		{"id":"a380775b697b6c29","type":"text","text":"- Brags that most students fail or that the class is \"hard to pass\"; treats high failure rates as a flex/badge of honor\n- Says things like \"that’s trivial\" or mocks students (even publicly) for not understanding; treats beginners like experts and lacks empathy; makes passive-aggressive or openly aggressive remarks\n- Complains about teaching, money, or not wanting to be there\n- Acts arrogant, superior, or dismissive of questions; blames students for basically everything","x":0,"y":2480,"width":640,"height":280},
		{"id":"3bf79dbd6331bbb9","type":"text","text":"Poor teaching techniques","x":720,"y":2320,"width":640,"height":100,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"6e55420f45d07374","type":"text","text":"- Reads directly off slides or PDFs without explaining\n- Uses someone else's slides\n\t- It's not bad if they're genuinely good slideshows, but most of the time they are just using them out of laziness and the slides are often outdated or even plain wrong\n- Tells students to \"just read it yourselves\"\n- Teaches out of order, jumps between chapters, or mixes topics incoherently (without a good reason to do so)\n- Goes on long, **irrelevant tangents**\n- Provides no examples or meaningful explanations\n- Assigns work unrelated (or marginally related) to what was taught\n- Makes exams far harder than most/all homework and assignments they gave out\n- Refuses to answer questions or says \"watch the video again\"\n- Uses cold calling aggressively or mockingly\n- Uses anti-scientific or factually wrong material *(it happens!)*\n- Forces note-taking at an unreasonable pace","x":720,"y":2480,"width":640,"height":520},
		{"id":"860439a9b2c07f06","type":"text","text":"Organization\nissues","x":1440,"y":2320,"width":640,"height":100,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"05528dd7812a1f58","type":"text","text":"- No syllabus, incomplete syllabus, or changes it constantly\n- Syllabus contradicts LMS (Canvas, Moodle, etc.)\n- LMS is disorganized or clearly neglected\n- Doesn't post grades or returns work **only at the end of the semester**\n- Says \"it's your job to track your grade, not mine\"\n- Forgets what was taught or confuses different classes, often\n- Mishandles \"extra credit\"\n- Gives unclear or missing instructions","x":1440,"y":2480,"width":640,"height":300},
		{"id":"dc5aa724b10d2949","type":"text","text":"Communication\nissues","x":2160,"y":2320,"width":640,"height":100,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"8eac15b120e5b65b","type":"text","text":"- Does not respond to emails (or is hostile when replying)\n- Gives vague, dismissive, or angry replies\n- Refuses to clarify assignments or policies\n- Doesn't accept feedback or *claims they never receive any*","x":2160,"y":2480,"width":640,"height":180},
		{"id":"d2b672efd12aec05","type":"text","text":"Assessment Issues","x":2880,"y":2320,"width":640,"height":100,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"091346db70ff52cb","type":"text","text":"- Exams don’t match lectures, homework, or reviews\n- Grades arbitrarily or inconsistently\n- Loses assignments or marks submitted work as missing\n- Penalizes students for trivial formatting issues\n- Threatens expulsion over minor technicalities\n- Uses peer grading unfairly as a major grade component\n- Changes grading rules mid-semester\n- Gives different rules to different students","x":2880,"y":2480,"width":640,"height":280},
		{"id":"0e526d710cb0f989","type":"text","text":"Professionalism\nissues","x":3600,"y":2320,"width":640,"height":100,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"2f9ebfeb50ea7214","type":"text","text":"- Publicly shames classes for being “the worst”\n- Talks excessively about their own career instead of teaching\n- Bullies students or talks about them behind their backs\n- Makes sexist or biased remarks\n- Uses fear, intimidation, or humiliation as \"motivation\"\n- Refuses accommodations or accessibility options\n- Encourages students to drop the course instead of helping them succeed","x":3600,"y":2480,"width":640,"height":260},
		{"id":"0892404cb36611fa","type":"text","text":"# 4 — Principles and experiments for a better system\n\n> [!lens] These are directions toward an ideal world, not a finished blueprint.\n\nTo skeptics who claim there cannot be a better system because we are at the peak of knowledge: **never before has there been as much knowledge as there is today**.\n\nLess than 1,000 years ago, it was normal to be a generalist. There were far fewer specialists because humanity knew much less about itself, the natural world, and science.\n\nThe current education system developed in a rush to handle this expanding volume of knowledge and produce specialists. Of course it is imperfect, and of course there can be better ways to respond.","x":5760,"y":1020,"width":1360,"height":340,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"0a17e9372dff2e35","type":"text","text":"### Evaluation should reveal understanding\n\n**Peer review.** Include review of work while accounting for bias, friendships, differences in knowledge, and relevance. Reviews should be anonymous and standardized enough that evaluators do not simply give everyone top grades. Strong students should not be pushed out of universities.\n\n**Frequent evidence.** Review assignments across broad topics and use random daily questions to check whether a student truly _understands_ the subject.\n\n**Discovery before formalization.** In physics, students could first frame the problem of falling, run experiments, and arrive at the value of _g_ before being told that it exists. Only then introduce how others approached the problem historically, and how gravity was named and formalized. That is the ideal-world version.\n\n**Flexible time.** Classes would not have fixed durations; they would continue until the audience—the students—votes to end them.\n\nA truly effective education system ultimately also requires changing how jobs work.","x":4320,"y":1420,"width":640,"height":480,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"52ffb2ebf9e212e2","type":"text","text":"### Teachers as willing mentors, not masters\n\nReduce the teacher-as-master hierarchy. Learners need people who know more, recognize that they do not have everything figured out, and genuinely **want** to teach.","x":5040,"y":1420,"width":640,"height":160},
		{"id":"80e7e717b80302d7","type":"text","text":"### Make growth the visible incentive\n\nGive everyone a clearer, stronger incentive to become smarter and improve at what they are studying.","x":5760,"y":1420,"width":640,"height":140},
		{"id":"11cceaf379455d0e","type":"text","text":"### Education in the age of AI\n\nIt may feel clichéd to say, but knowledge work has changed permanently. One-to-one learning has changed too: it is no longer unfathomable for every student to have an infinitely patient tutor that knows every subject well, works individually with them, and adapts to their pace.","x":6480,"y":1420,"width":640,"height":200},
		{"id":"05bd6072ec6f246c","type":"text","text":"### Before higher education: preserve structure, improve the foundations\n\nUp to high school, I think the system is mostly fine and has no major flaws. Children need **quite a bit** of structure and need to learn how to behave. Most of that should be the parents’ responsibility, not teachers’, but school still has a role.\n\n#### A possible sequence\n\n- **1st–4th grade — the basics:** ages 6–9.\n- **5th–8th grade — the essentials:** ages 10–13.\n- **9th–11th grade:** ages 14–17.\n\nI will not focus heavily on education before high school. It could be better, but it is not terrible. This stage needs strong structure and fundamentals:\n- Mathematics.\n- Some science and physics.\n- English.\n- Physical education.\n- Philosophy.\n- Logic and programming.\n- A creative practice such as drawing, cooking, music, writing, theater, or using software such as GIMP.\n\n#### Physical education and nutrition\n\n- At the beginning of a period, students could choose a sport they enjoy and commit to it.\n- Everyone in the school could have physical education at the same time.\n- Begin with basic running and strength or endurance work, then let each student choose from several sports.\n- Teach more basic nutrition.","x":5760,"y":1960,"width":640,"height":820},
		{"id":"be6c1b210a74e5a0","type":"text","text":"### A small future pilot\n\n> [!idea] One way to validate these directions would be a tiny pilot—even 30 students—before attempting to scale. Invite students finishing high school who genuinely want to learn and are willing to spend a gap year experimenting with a different model.","x":4320,"y":1960,"width":640,"height":260},
		{"id":"9f1b6a7e87d21e57","type":"text","text":"### Ask students why they chose university\n\nCreate an anonymous questionnaire. Ask participants to be completely honest, with nothing to hide, and to reconstruct what they truly thought when making the decision:\n- Why did you come to university?\n- Why did you choose _this_ university?\n- Why did you choose this degree or program?\n- Did you consider working or trying other things first? Why or why not?\n- Do you think it was the right decision? If you could go back, would you choose the same path?","x":4320,"y":2280,"width":640,"height":340},
		{"id":"595f1943a3c3f8d9","type":"text","text":"### Research cue\n\nLook into Germany's **Duale Ausbildung** model.","x":5040,"y":1960,"width":640,"height":100},
		{"id":"f772988de899c2ba","type":"text","text":"### Models and initiatives worth studying\n\n- [42 School](https://www.42network.org/about-us/mission/)\n- [Open Source Society University](https://ossu.dev/)\n- [Math Academy](https://www.mathacademy.com/)\n- [Astra Nova](https://www.astranova.org/)\n- [3Blue1Brown](https://www.youtube.com/@3blue1brown)\n- [Khan Academy](https://www.khanacademy.org/)\n- [Alpha School](https://alpha.school/)","x":5040,"y":2120,"width":640,"height":300},
		{"id":"2318e41e247138bf","type":"link","url":"https://hilariusbookbinder.substack.com/p/the-average-college-student-today","x":6480,"y":1960,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"aebdeb7997367e7f","type":"link","url":"https://medium.com/my-pewsonal-bwog/standardized-tests-too-crude-to-be-used-7afb0715a3e2","x":7200,"y":1960,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"5e47b2d1b4ddd288","type":"text","text":"# 5 — Research notebook and outside perspectives\n\nThese notes preserve arguments straight from videos and articles, including claims I have not independently verified or endorsed. Sources sit beside the notes they informed.","x":0,"y":3060,"width":1360,"height":180,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"64b2b19fcb76139c","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/W1QZwzGlwlQ","x":0,"y":3300,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"9e43197e4aea4fdd","type":"text","text":"### Source notes — cooperation, lifelong learning, and the future of universities\n\n> The linked source argues:\n\n#### Research and education\n\n- **Research** is discovering something nobody knew before.\n- **Education** is discovering something you did not know before.\n- The source presents universities as the institutions that combine both roles.\n\n#### 1. Replace competition with cooperation\n\nUniversities compete for:\n- Students.\n- The best teachers and researchers.\n- Fast discoveries and publications.\n- Funding, which feeds a cycle of more research, prestige, and students.\n\nThe source argues that this competition-based approach should become a cooperation-based one.\n\n#### 2. Higher education should not primarily prepare people for a vocation\n\n- We do not know which jobs will exist in the future, so creativity, adaptability, and the ability to change matter.\n- Universities should develop critical thinking, writing, and productive cooperation rather than being dominated by specialized, hands-on vocational skills.\n- Many vocational skills can be learned on the job.\n- Higher education could support lifelong learning interwoven with work, with shorter university visits and learning as you go.\n\n#### 3. Do more on sustainability\n\nStudents expect more, and society needs more.\n\n> [!quote] Micro-courses on specific topics or skills will become a normal part of working life. Universities must adapt or risk irrelevance as the market for full-time students shrinks.","x":0,"y":3720,"width":640,"height":1040},
		{"id":"95fa05c1ef37ed6d","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/i3w3_w9G1n0","x":720,"y":3300,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"6eda5f9dcb7017fa","type":"text","text":"### Source notes — school, stress, and the lost joy of learning\n\n> The linked source argues:\n\n- The school system causes students stress and anxiety and **kills their passion for learning**.\n- School should help students develop an intellectual mindset and a passion for learning.\n- Students should conduct their own research into topics that catch their interest and curiosity; children might then enjoy reading.\n- School is currently more about *passing* than learning.\n- The speaker says he mastered *short-term memorization*—cramming for tests—because that is what school rewards.\n- Schools are designed to send students to college.\n\n> [!poem]\n> I always hated school\n> And I’m not saying that\n> Just to look cool\n> For 13 years of my life, I sat\n>\n> In those chairs\n> And played along\n> Acutely aware\n> That I didn’t belong\n>\n> In the broken system\n> That kills the joy for learning\n> Intelligence vs wisdom\n> A young mind yearning\n>\n> To create\n> Without the restrictions\n> A constant state\n> Of contradiction\n>\n> Standardized testing\n> To prove that you’re smart\n> No time for resting\n> Forget about art\n>\n> Pointless rules\n> And obligations\n> Lack of tools\n> For compensation\n>\n> Producing productive members of\n> Society\n> This isn’t a one-size-fits-all glove;\n> Variety\n>\n> Is what they need\n> Show them their options\n> Then let them succeed\n> Free from the toxins\n>\n> Of our broken education system","x":720,"y":3720,"width":640,"height":1560},
		{"id":"fbf101ecb3140d67","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/tDGWLXf2vRE","x":1440,"y":3300,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"43c8896d02f29981","type":"text","text":"### Source notes — one proposed redesign for school\n\n> The first section comes straight from the linked video; the later proposal comes from a comment.\n\n#### Teachers\n\n- Teacher quality is hit or miss.\n- Consider paying teachers more.\n\n#### Sleep and schedules\n\n- 7:30 a.m. is too early; school should start at 9 a.m.\n- Reintroduce roughly 30 minutes of nap time.\n\n#### Drawing\n\n- Do not punish students for drawing.\n\n#### Four levels of freedom\n\n1. No phone; constant supervision; mandatory school-provided tutoring funded by the extra resources. Other students may also attend that tutoring.\n2. Roughly how school works now.\n3. The default: listen to music, use computers freely, and have no mandatory homework.\n4. Leave your classes or visit other classes when you want, and do anything non-disruptive, while still remaining at school three quarters of the time.\n\n- A student removed from level 4 must wait a whole semester to regain it.\n- Grades use A, A-, B, B-, C, and so on. The average generally corresponds to the four freedom levels—A/A- to level 4, B/B- to level 3, C/C- to level 2—but behavior can change the level.\n- If the teacher finishes the day’s material, students are free to leave.\n- The video also renames grade levels, but that is not particularly relevant here.\n\n#### A comment adds another proposal\n\n- Use project-based learning so assignments have a purpose rather than being random problems.\n- Homework is a learning tool and does not always improve grades. Make only work that directly supports the project—such as a script—mandatory.\n- Work submitted within three days should retain full credit, unless a different rule was stated; students can also ask for an extension.\n- Use more hands-on lessons for tactile learners.\n- Hold easier tests once a week, on Fridays. Use them as learning benchmarks to decide whether the class needs more time or can move forward.\n\n> [!scope] This proposal is for school through the end of high school, not university, although some ideas may still transfer.","x":1440,"y":3720,"width":640,"height":1380},
		{"id":"ffecdb8cf8377855","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/O-QbVkyi8yc","x":2160,"y":3300,"width":300,"height":169},
		{"id":"9eb697401f112b16","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/2IyJIZHgRvw","x":2500,"y":3300,"width":300,"height":169},
		{"id":"347689399f21302a","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/YoTc8LgY-RA","x":2160,"y":3509,"width":300,"height":169},
		{"id":"f06a17c610a91a55","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/FCu_Yu9EwOw","x":2500,"y":3509,"width":300,"height":169},
		{"id":"36e3f5b83a229b92","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/IG2zSQ27VFQ","x":2160,"y":3718,"width":300,"height":169},
		{"id":"4c6801863bd002c9","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/YNQDp3v-VGE","x":2500,"y":3718,"width":300,"height":169},
		{"id":"005f24422aaddb04","type":"text","text":"### Source synthesis — purpose, problem-first learning, pacing, and real-life skills\n\n> These notes synthesize several videos in the source strip above.\n\n#### Explain why a subject matters\n\n- Teachers often do not explain why children are learning a subject. Students are dropped into mathematics wondering: “Why am I learning this?”, “What is the point?”, and “Why am I being asked to solve these strange problems?”\n- The **why** is extremely important. Our brains evolved to discard information that seems irrelevant.\n- School often replaces relevance with punishment: remember the apparently random formulas or suffer the consequences.\n\n#### Teach through a problem, not by presenting tools first\n\n- Choose a problem, then use mathematics, physics, economics, and other tools to solve it.\n- Example: take apart an engine, understand it, and put it back together. In the process, students learn why they need wrenches, screwdrivers, and the other tools.\n- Solving the problem makes each tool’s relevance obvious.\n\n#### A degree is an indicator, not proof of ability\n\n- A college degree may be an indicator, but it is not necessary in every case; the notes point to Bill Gates, Larry Ellison, and Steve Jobs.\n- Much of the world’s knowledge is now available almost for free.\n- College still has signaling and social value:\n  - It can show that somebody works hard and soldiers through annoying assignments regardless of mood.\n  - It lets people spend time with peers before entering the workforce.\n  - In this framing, college is partly “for fun” and proof that you can do your chores—not primarily for learning.\n  - Tesla is cited as asking for demonstrable experience rather than a degree.\n\n#### Make learning engaging and individually paced\n\n- Learning should not feel like one huge chore. Gamify the process of putting data and algorithms into the brain.\n- Children can play video games on autopilot for hours; learning can be similarly interactive and engaging.\n- Disconnect a single level or grade from every subject. Let each person progress at the fastest pace they can sustain or want for each subject.\n- Repeated, unenthusiastic lectures transmit the lecturer’s lack of excitement. Students then do not know why they are there. Children need purpose.\n\n#### Ad Astra and Astra Nova\n\n- Elon Musk created Ad Astra, later spun out as [Astra Nova](https://www.astranova.org/).\n- There were no conventional year grades; children of different ages learned together.\n- Learning was tailored to each child’s rate, aptitudes, and abilities.\n- Teaching began with problems rather than tools.\n- The children reportedly loved going to school.\n- The approach emphasized first-principles thinking:\n  - Analyze the physics and economics of a rocket and ask whether it is physically possible.\n  - Do not be constrained by what has already been done; return to the drawing board.\n- Critical thinking was central.\n\n#### School often teaches the opposite of real-life success\n\nThe notes attribute this contrast to Kim Kiyosaki:\n- “Do not make mistakes.”\n- “Do as you are told.”\n- “Take tests alone; do not cooperate.”\n- “Do it by yourself.”\n- “There is only one right answer.”\n\nThe source argues that these rules are the opposite of what real life rewards.\n\n#### Teach more about money","x":2160,"y":3947,"width":640,"height":2040},
		{"id":"72c7b2f82975e03c","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/MDzS4ed6tgk","x":2880,"y":3300,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"a7bb225664376221","type":"text","text":"### Source notes — a traditionalist critique of modern universities\n\n> The claims and prescriptions below belong to the linked source and are preserved for investigation, not presented here as verified facts.\n\n#### What education used to be, in the source’s account\n\nFor roughly 2,500 years, education centered on:\n- Cultivating the soul or spirit.\n- Teaching virtues and how to live a good life.\n- Understanding how knowledge connects.\n- Putting the humanities—philosophy, history, languages, literature, and art—at the center.\n\nThe source argues that there is now no agreed purpose beyond “school → grades → job.”\n\n#### 1. Mass education\n\nThe source claims that:\n- Mass education began in the 1800s to produce obedient factory workers.\n- It became job training rather than cultivation of the soul.\n- Content was watered down to teach 100 times more people.\n- A 1913 eighth-grade test supposedly defeats Ivy League students today.\n- Everyone now “needs” college while degrees are worth less.\n- People take on substantial debt for degrees their jobs do not require.\n\n#### 2. Hyper-specialization\n\n- The mid-1800s brought an explosion of knowledge, especially in science.\n- The response was to turn everyone into a narrow expert.\n- “Jack of all trades” changed from a compliment into an insult.\n- The humanities developed an inferiority complex and copied scientific hyper-specialization, even where it makes little sense for literature or history.\n- Professors write inaccessible jargon about extremely narrow topics.\n- Disciplines do not talk to one another.\n- The result includes meaningless work that nobody can challenge.\n\n#### 3. Oppressor–oppressed ideology\n\nThe source claims that:\n- Marxist oppressor–oppressed analysis spread over 50–70 years and accelerated in the 1980s and 1990s.\n- Some professors introduce themselves as Marxist feminists.\n- Race, class, gender, and oppressor-versus-oppressed framing displace open-ended inquiry.\n- The motivation becomes an ideological axe to grind rather than knowledge.\n- The “dead white males” of classical education become the enemy.\n- Calling oneself Marxist is accepted while calling oneself fascist is not, although the source thinks both should sound equally bad.\n\n#### 4. Bureaucratic bloat\n\n- The past 30 years brought an explosion of administrators rather than teachers.\n- Administrators can earn more than professors.\n- Tuition rose far faster than general inflation; the source gives an illustrative jump from $10,000 to $100,000 per year.\n\n#### 5. DEI and culture wars\n\nThe source claims that:\n- Many universities require DEI statements.\n- The atmosphere can feel Kafkaesque or Orwellian.\n- Cancel culture, safe spaces, and reduced freedom of speech make people afraid to say what they think.\n- “Each time you stay silent, it eats away at your soul a bit more.”\n- This is the opposite of teaching virtue and strength and creates a toxic environment.\n\n#### 6. Non-academic programs\n\n- Colleges teach hands-on job skills such as hotel front-desk management.\n- The source argues that these do not belong in academic institutions.\n- Such programs contribute to bloat and watered-down education.\n\n#### Proposed responses\n\n**Short term**\n- Endure the system if a degree is necessary.\n- Find the institution that best preserves older ideals.\n- The source sees access to great libraries as the only clear advantage.\n\n**Let weak institutions implode**\n- There are too many schools and too few students.\n- Many institutions, especially newer ones, will fail.\n- The source sees that failure as necessary to clear out low-quality institutions.\n\n**Use alumni pressure**\n- Write to your alma mater: “You educated me with these values; now you are abandoning them.”\n\n**Build private academies**\n- Return to a Plato-and-Aristotle model of studying with a master teacher.\n- Use online learning to make the model feasible.\n- Focus on great books, classical education, Latin and Greek, and an environment without bureaucracy, DEI, or culture wars.\n- Make **lifelong learning** the key innovation:\n  - Do not cram everything in by age 22.\n  - Spend one or two hours each week reading difficult books throughout life.\n  - Pursue projects that span decades.\n- These academies become “keepers of the flame” if universities implode.","x":2880,"y":3720,"width":640,"height":2540},
		{"id":"7f7b3d2388d04292","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/5qpXypoVx1M","x":3600,"y":3300,"width":300,"height":169},
		{"id":"02e87a4914c19f5b","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/EqVO7NF9VOE","x":3940,"y":3300,"width":300,"height":169},
		{"id":"c2fabea0103da462","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/dqTTojTija8","x":3600,"y":3509,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"2ae928def8c5fd1d","type":"text","text":"### Surveillance as normalization\n\nOne concern: conditioning young people to accept being watched 24/7.","x":3600,"y":3929,"width":640,"height":120},
		{"id":"353d4b0ee6388e23","type":"text","text":"# 6 — For students: navigating the current system\n\nThese approaches intentionally stay separate: my method, an external guide, and community anecdotes sometimes disagree.","x":4320,"y":3060,"width":2080,"height":160,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"6a114ffee589e9ff","type":"text","text":"### My personal study advice\n\n> This is how I navigate the **current** education system when the practical goal is a strong university grade. It is personal, not universal.\n\n#### What studying means to me\n\n- Studying is not mysterious; it is learning material well enough to answer questions on a test.\n- Do not fear a subject. It is a collection of simpler parts.\n- Engage with the material day to day: attend class, pay attention, and watch related videos in free moments such as a subway ride.\n\n#### What people often do wrong\n\n- Study for too long in one session—more than two or three hours.\n- Study for too many hours in one day—more than five.\n- Multitask; it does not work.\n- Sacrifice sleep, even though sleep is crucial for learning.\n- Reject every other commitment because exams are coming. A day has roughly 16 waking hours, and you will not study for all 16; three extra hours will rarely transform the grade.\n\n#### How I study\n\n- Start at most three to five days before the exam.\n- **Always** do past and mock exams; prioritize them over worksheets.\n- Solve quickly:\n  - If you cannot solve something and know you are stuck, inspect the solution and learn from it.\n  - If you already know it, move on.\n- Do not write summaries unless formulas genuinely need memorization. Spend about 10 minutes checking somebody else’s summary for omissions.\n- Have a friend who understands more—and be that friend for someone else. Explaining exposes doubts and reveals where apparent deep understanding was only memorization.\n\n#### During the exam\n\n- Leave the hardest questions for last, even if that temporarily means skipping half the paper.\n- Complete a fast first pass, then review.\n- Eat chocolate or another sugary food beforehand as a personal ritual.\n- Never leave an answer blank:\n  - Think like the teacher.\n  - In multiple-choice questions, eliminate options and choose the most likely remaining answer.\n\n#### Techniques I recommend but do not usually use\n\n- Active recall: force the brain to retrieve the answer.\n- Study a little and often.\n- Use several senses: see, hear, and discuss the material. I do this occasionally.","x":5760,"y":3280,"width":640,"height":1440,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"5c9688bb58730256","type":"link","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45635533","x":4320,"y":3280,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"322e4c52f574d14d","type":"text","text":"### Community study anecdotes and disagreements\n\n#### The “cheating” method\n\n- A student tried writing a cheat sheet on his arm, ran out of space, and rewrote it several times.\n- He memorized everything in the process: “I hid it all in my head! The professor didn’t even know I was cheating.”\n\n#### Study socializing\n\n- One person joined study groups to spend time with people he found attractive.\n- He had to learn enough to help and impress them—and eventually met his wife.\n\n#### Predict the lecture\n\n- Try to predict what the professor will say or write next.\n- Prediction keeps some people engaged and makes the material feel self-discovered.\n- For them, it works better than note-taking.\n\n#### Note-taking is not universal\n\n- Some people’s minds go blank while taking notes.\n- For them, 100% attention and no notes works better.\n- Writing can distract from understanding.\n\n#### Teaching is learning\n\n- Explaining reveals gaps you did not know you had.\n- Helping less advanced students forces genuine understanding.\n\n#### Interest changes everything\n\n- When the brain finds a topic interesting, absorbing knowledge can feel unavoidable.\n- Some people cannot force themselves to learn what does not interest them.\n- That makes school brutal but can make self-directed real life easier.\n\n#### Study environments differ\n\n- Quiet libraries make some people zone out.\n- Busy coffee shops with headphones create deep focus for others.\n- Universal “find a quiet place” advice makes people with different needs feel like failures.\n\n#### Consistency compounds\n\n- Working 10% more each day compounds over a lifetime.\n- Small daily steps beat occasional heroic efforts.\n- Regularity can beat intelligence.\n\n#### Teach people how to learn\n\n- High school can be too easy to require real study skills.\n- University then arrives and students are unprepared.\n- “Learning to learn” should be taught explicitly.\n\n#### The university-agency problem\n\n- Students have little agency and follow arbitrary rules.\n- University feels simultaneously low-stakes—nobody cares—and high-stakes because it shapes the future, producing cynicism.\n- The feeling that life is no longer merely a game may arrive only after graduation.\n\n#### Sleep, cramming, coffee, and all-nighters\n\n- Sleep commits memories and can make problems easier the next morning.\n- If you have never practiced that kind of problem, however, cramming may beat sleeping.\n- Coffee and food two hours before the test can help; coffee immediately before or during stress can hurt.\n- All-nighters can damage grades while still creating intense social bonds and friendships.\n\n#### Incentives and tactics\n\n- Grading curves can turn 50% into 75% and encourage students to game course selection rather than learn.\n- Visiting office hours without a real question can feel manipulative. Professors notice insincerity, yet the people who show up often do better.\n- Do not spend 90% of the time reading about learning and 10% practicing. The basic loop is read or listen, then apply and question, and repeat; many branded learning methods are pseudo-science.\n- Never leave a test early. Rechecking almost always reveals mistakes, costs nothing, and has large upside.","x":4320,"y":3700,"width":640,"height":1960},
		{"id":"f030f43cd80f1c6b","type":"link","url":"https://cs.stanford.edu/people/karpathy/advice.html","x":5040,"y":3280,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"bed5bde0fd9f5c97","type":"text","text":"### External study advice — Andrej Karpathy\n\n#### General\n\n- All-nighters are not worth it. Sleep matters; maximize the number of nights your brain spends with the material.\n- Attend tutorials or review sessions. Simply thinking about the material already helps.\n\n#### Prepare for the test\n\n- Create a study schedule. List everything you need to know and time-box it so the early material does not consume all your time.\n- Look at previous tests **before** you start studying.\n- Reading and understanding are not the same as reproducing the material. Be able to write down the important parts and re-derive them at will.\n- Collaborate with others, but study alone first and collaborate nearer the end.\n- Do not spend time only with stronger students.\n- Visit the professor’s office hours at least once before the final exam.\n- Study in advance: roughly three days for midterms and six for exams.\n- In an emergency, if things are going badly and exhaustion takes over, chug an energy drink—the note frames this bluntly as chemistry.\n- For mathematics-like subjects, exercises beat reading.\n- Make yourself a cheat sheet.\n- Study where other people are also studying, even on different subjects; their focus creates social pressure not to slack off.\n\n#### On test day\n\n- About two hours before the test, have coffee and food.\n- Study intensely immediately before the test to use short-term memory.\n\n#### During the test\n\n- Use pencil so bad attempts can be erased.\n- Scan every question briefly before beginning.\n- Do the easy questions first; harder ones may become easier after warming up.\n- Keep the work neat—the marker is a tired human being.\n- Box or circle the final answer and think from the marker’s perspective.\n- Never leave early; staying has almost no cost and checking has substantial upside.\n- Communicate with the marker and show what you know, not only a bare answer.\n- Prioritize using the points assigned to each question.\n- With fewer than five minutes left, stop wrestling with one problem. Re-read the paper, find missed sub-questions, and ensure everything has an answer.\n\n#### Final perspective\n\n- Nobody will care about your grades unless they are bad.\n- Time is scarce. Reach the point where you do not fail tests, then shift attention to more important work.\n- Value recommendation letters from well-known professors.\n- Beyond research projects, join or start side projects. Contribute to open source, build or improve a library, create something worthwhile, document it, and blog about it. Those artifacts will matter more than grades a few years later.","x":5040,"y":3700,"width":640,"height":1800}
	],
